## Solution

We seek the residue of $f(z) = \dfrac{1}{e^{e^{1/z}}} = e^{-e^{1/z}}$ at $z=0$, an essential singularity.

**Substitution.** Set $w = 1/z$, so $z = 1/w$. The Laurent expansion of $f(z)$ about $z=0$ is

$$f(z) = e^{-e^{1/z}} = e^{-e^{w}}, \qquad w = 1/z.$$

If $f(z) = \sum_{k} a_k\, z^{k}$ is the Laurent series in $z$, then substituting $z = 1/w$ gives $f(1/w) = \sum_k a_k\, w^{-k}$. In particular, the coefficient $a_{-1}$ (the residue at $z=0$) equals the coefficient of $w^{1}$ in the **Taylor** expansion of $e^{-e^{w}}$ about $w=0$.

**Compute the Taylor coefficient.** Write $g(w) = e^{-e^{w}} = \sum_{m=0}^{\infty} c_m w^{m}$. Then

$$c_1 = g'(0).$$

Differentiating,

$$g'(w) = -e^{w}\, e^{-e^{w}},$$

so

$$c_1 = g'(0) = -e^{0}\, e^{-e^{0}} = -1 \cdot e^{-1} = -\frac{1}{e}.$$

**Verification via direct Laurent expansion.** For $z\neq 0$,

$$e^{-e^{1/z}} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{n}}{n!}\, e^{n/z} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{n}}{n!} \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{n^{k}}{k!}\, z^{-k}.$$

The double series is absolutely convergent for every $z\neq 0$, since

$$\sum_{n\ge 0}\frac{1}{n!}\sum_{k\ge 0}\frac{n^{k}}{k!}|z|^{-k} = \sum_{n\ge 0}\frac{e^{n/|z|}}{n!} = e^{e^{1/|z|}} < \infty,$$

so termwise collection is justified. The coefficient of $z^{-1}$ (i.e. $k=1$) is

$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{n}}{n!}\cdot n = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{n}}{(n-1)!} = \sum_{m=0}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}}{m!} = -e^{-1}.$$

Both methods agree.

$$\boxed{\operatorname{Res}_{z=0}\,\frac{1}{e^{e^{1/z}}} = -\frac{1}{e}}$$

